Rollback saves and loads copy only the RDRAM pages written since

RMG-K's session (rmgk_gekko.cpp with GekkoNet, limited_saving off) saves the state after every
frame into GekkoNet's slot for that frame, the frames re-run after a rollback included: a rollback
of D frames is one load, D re-run frames each followed by a save, then the real frame and its save.
Every save and load copied all 8 MB of RDRAM, on a Switch about 2.0 ms a save and 2.4 ms a load, so
a 7-frame rollback spent about 2.4 + 8 x 2.0 = 18 ms copying. A frame of a match writes about 100 of
RDRAM's 2,048 pages of 4 KB.

The core now keeps a record of the RDRAM pages written since the last rollback save or load
(device/rdram/rdram.h: rdram_written, or rdram_written_all when a writer cannot say where):
- the memory handler (write_rdram_dram: every CPU write that is not the recompiler's direct store,
  and the interpreters' writes), the PI, SI and SP DMAs, invalidate_r4300_cached_code (cheats; a
  whole invalidation, as at a reset or a state load, counts as everything), the cheat engine;
- the recompiler: its stores to RDRAM (kseg0) check invalid_code[page] != 1 after the store and call
  invalidate_block() if so, which is how a write to compiled code is caught. After each rollback save
  or load every page without code is set to 2 ("armed", new_dynarec_rdram_arm), so the first store to
  it calls invalidate_block(), which notes the page and sets it back to 1, and nothing else happens:
  the page behaves as any page without code. Everything in C that reads invalid_code only tests it
  for zero, so 2 and 1 mean the same to it. Pages with code trap every store already, and
  invalidate_block() notes the page it invalidates. With the TLB in use the recompiler's stores go
  through memory_map instead, so then every save and load counts all of RDRAM as written;
- plugins: the RSP plugin reports its writes (ZnxRdramWritten, patches/0015) and says so
  (ZnxRdramWriterReports); a video plugin that never writes RDRAM says so (the null one does it
  itself; for GLideN64 with frame buffer emulation off the front end does, game/game_core.c). Until
  a plugin has said it, each of its calls that can write (DoRspCycles, ProcessDList, ProcessRDPList,
  ShowCFB, UpdateScreen, FBRead, ViStatusChanged, ViWidthChanged) counts as having written all of
  RDRAM; so does every audio list handed to the audio plugin. Reset when a plugin is attached;
- the front end reports what it writes through its own pointer (ZnxRdramWritten; bench_core.c's
  forced-match setup).

Rollback slots (main/savestates.c). The core remembers the buffers it has saved into (up to 32, by
address and a tag the save writes into the header: header[6..7], flag rollback_state_flag_has_tag,
header size 8 words; loaders take the header size from header[1], so the rest of the layout is
unchanged and spectator keyframes, made by savestates_save_full_buffer, keep the old 6-word header).
For each it keeps the set of pages in which the buffer may differ from RDRAM: at each rollback save
or load the pages written since the last one are added to every slot's set; a save into a slot
copies only that set's pages of RDRAM (everything else in the state is written in full, as before)
and empties it; a load from a slot copies only its set's pages into RDRAM, empties it and adds it
to every other slot's set. Every page outside the set is equal already, so the buffer, and the
machine after a load, are byte for byte what a whole copy gives. A buffer whose tag is not the one
the core wrote last (changed or reallocated by someone else), a buffer the core has not saved into,
or a different state length, is saved and loaded whole as before. Patch 0005's comparison of code
pages before a load now only looks at the pages the load copies.

ZnxRollbackVerify(1) (znx-bench --rollback-verify) checks it as it runs: after every save into a
slot a whole save is made into a scratch buffer and compared with it byte for byte (but the tag);
after every load from a slot likewise against the slot; and at every save or load each RDRAM page
that changed since the last one without being noted is counted (a copy of RDRAM is kept for that).
ZnxRollbackStats reads the counters.

Measured (znx-bench --rollback-stress 10 7 over 9,000 frames with the mash script, which now saves
after every frame and every re-run frame into a ring of slots as the session does; Mac M5): a save
118 -> 14 us, a load 154 -> 26 us, about 90 pages copied per save and per load; the 7-frame rollback
with its saves 1.75 -> 0.87 ms, of which the re-run frames are now 0.75 ms. Every RDRAM hash and
check line of the harness is unchanged, and so is every re-run frame.

