+/* ---- Z-Cancel NX: rollback slots ----
+ *
+ * The netplay session saves the state after every frame, re-run ones included, into a ring of
+ * buffers it owns (GekkoNet's slots, handed over as the preallocated buffer of a rollback save) and
+ * loads one of them for a rollback. Each such save or load used to copy all 8 MB of RDRAM, about
+ * 2 ms each on a Switch, while a frame writes about 100 of RDRAM's 2048 pages.
+ *
+ * The core now remembers the buffers it has saved into (up to ROLLBACK_SLOT_MAX). For each it keeps
+ * the set of RDRAM pages in which the buffer may differ from RDRAM now ("stale" pages): the pages
+ * written since the buffer was saved (device/rdram/rdram.h keeps the pages written since the last
+ * rollback save or load; at each save or load they are added to every slot's set and cleared),
+ * plus, after a load, the pages in which the loaded state differed. A save into a known buffer copies
+ * only its stale pages of RDRAM and writes everything else in full, as before; a load from one
+ * copies only its stale pages into RDRAM. Every other byte of RDRAM is already equal, so the buffer
+ * and the restored machine are byte for byte what a whole copy gives (--rollback-verify checks it).
+ *
+ * A buffer is known by its address and a tag the save writes into the header (header[6..7], with
+ * rollback_state_flag_has_tag; the header is then 8 words: loaders read the header size from
+ * header[1], so the layout after it is unchanged). A buffer with another tag, or none, was changed
+ * or reallocated by someone else: it is saved and loaded whole, as is any buffer when the record of
+ * written pages cannot be trusted (the TLB in use, a plugin that writes RDRAM without saying where:
+ * then rdram_written_all is set at every save or load). */
+enum { ROLLBACK_SLOT_MAX = 32, ROLLBACK_PAGE_WORDS = RDRAM_PAGE_COUNT / 64 };
